 know  budgets can only stretch so far and you  can see the nhs saying  either it can't meet those targets which  pritchard at the moment is saying it can  um  or  just trying to to push back against any  suggestion for from ministers  and obviously currently i believe steve  barkley is still the health secretary  he's very into efficiencies um that they  might try to squeeze things further  well i was going to say it's difficult  to talk about what the government thinks  when it comes to the nhs seeing as the  government is currently collapsing all  around us  but ministers previous to this current  upset in westminster and whoever forms  the next government going forward do you  think they're waking up to the realities  facing the nhs  uh  yes and no um  so i i think um  that the treasury  is not um and the reason i say that is  that um it had blocked uh very  forcefully  any attempts to do proper workforce  planning and which would  in the long term  help the nhs to be more efficient  because it is hugely inefficient if you  have massive gaps on your rotors and if  you have  so many vacancies within the health  service if you  cannot actually  perform  the operations that you need to because  you just don't have the nurses you don't  have the doctors um and so on and uh  and this is this is a sort of ongoing  problem um in terms of the uh the other  aspect of reality which is i think a  point that you make um  a fair bit cake which is about the  actual sustainability of the nhs as it  is i mean politically that is really  really difficult and recently there was  a paper that tony blair published and  that argued uh that the current model uh  is not sustainable and i have to say  having uh just finished writing a  history of the nhs for its 75th birthday  um  i've heard that phrase so many times i  think it starts to appear from the sort  of late 1960s onwards that the beven and  beveridge model uh no longer works in  today's society um the problem is is  that the solutions to that um there are  the um sort of outlier solutions  of scrapping it and going for something  completely different insurance-based  model um which is not only in america  but in other countries in europe and  does work uh well for uh people in in  europe so we you know it's always worth  pointing out that it's not uh the uk or  america and those are the only two  models in the whole world we do have a  slightly bigger world than that um  or changing um some of the structures  within the health service and this is  the the point that tony blair has been  making that he wants to  basically collapse the central control  over the health service and give  integrated care boards which are  responsible for the commissioning and  provision of nhs services  control over pretty much everything and  that's just not going to work with the  dna of the nhs which you know was set up  um to be a highly centralized highly  controlled  system that was one of bevan's  objectives in nationalising the  hospitals  it was one of the things that he said  himself that he would be the chief  receiver of complaints and ever since  then whether health secretaries have  legally been responsible for this or  legally distanced from that they have  always ended up talking about the  bedpans that have been dropped in  hospitals hundreds of miles away they  have always ended up forcing out chief  executives who aren't performing whether  or not that's their legal duty i mean  there's a quote by alan johnson for  instance um about mid-staffs where uh he  had civil servants buzzing around him  saying oh you can't do this you can't do  that and he just turned around and said  piss off i'm dealing with this um and  that's that you know that's ultimately  what it comes down to is the public  expect ministers to answer questions on  everything when it comes to the health  service and i don't quite see how that's  going to change  the the spectator data hub is keeping  track of public satisfaction with the  nhs and at the moment there are actually  more people who say they're quite or  very dissatisfied with its performance  compared to those who are satisfied but  what should we read into those quite  headline statistics  yeah so there's there's lots of  interesting dynamics there one is the  political dynamic of who do the public  blame and they tend to blame the  politicians who they think are  um  are underfunding not supporting the  health service  properly there's also the  thing that  particularly  the later labour years the politicians  then really picked up which was the but  we're lucky dynamics so  you get people saying oh the nhs is in a  terrible state but we're lucky with our  local hospital and sort of still  uh bought into it at a local level which  can be um  hugely frustrating and disruptive  for anyone trying to embark on sensible  reforms because actually sometimes  people aren't lucky with their local  hospital and it would make more sense to  close you know the accident in emergency  into centralized services but  you never get a hospital closure that  the local politician or indeed that the  local  sort of vocal population are are going  to support and say you end up with  services remaining  even when it's not particularly  economical to do so  isabel in your upcoming book you  demonstrate how the nhs has been a  battleground for some of the fiercest  political contests of our time  as we look forward to an election  possibly in 2024 maybe sooner or indeed  a leadership election just within the  conservative party how is the nhs going  to figure  i think it will figure very highly uh  because you are already getting  headlines about the number of people who  are dying while on nhs waiting lists  you're seeing this really interesting  phenomenon of people crowdfunding for  private medical treatment so  um private medical insurance has gone up  and down depending on who's in  government the state of the nhs but  actually people who are paying upfront  rather than taking out insurance  for an operation that they need but have  been told they have a year to wait for  and in that time they worry that they're  going to get sicker or that their  quality of life is going to plummet they  just can't wait in pain for that long so  they're going on to these gofundme sites  and so on to try to cover their their  private health care um themselves and  so  when i talk to people in the health  service and when i talk to switched on  politicians about this they see the nhs  as being in  it's probably its greatest existential  crisis since its foundation even greater  than um at the the turn of the century  in 2000 when uh people like alan milburn  tony blair were genuinely worried the  public support for the nhs was going to  was just going to disappear public  consent was was going to disappoint  you'd get middle classes um opting out  and the consent of the middle classes  was really important at the time of the  foundation of the health service um  and so we're back in that place again  now uh you're gonna have a lot of people  getting very upset um in you know  hustings uh in election question times  and so on about their mother who has  been you know left to die by four  cancelled operations which was one of  the things the mavis skeet case that  that prompted a big revolution in health  service back in 2000  and that said  it isn't necessarily the case that the  nhs uh  always wins elections four parties and  that's the risk for labour is that they  could end up talking about the nhs uh  while not actually reassuring the public  or on anything else if you look at the  1992 election where the nhs um was a key  part of labour's campaign didn't win  them that election so uh so in terms of  a political battleground um  uh for actually winning an election i  think there's a there's a caveat there  but it will feature really heavily in  the in the campaign one last question  for you isabel on the politics 